00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:58.600
The two-stage TFSA-RRSP arbitrage exploits the fact that RRSP contributions generate larger

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:05.500
tax refunds when made in higher marginal brackets, while TFSA-ROOM can be preserved for later

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.960
lower bracket years. With fast-growing incomes common among mid-career Canadians,

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:16.260
the approach involves maxing RRSP contributions in peak earning years,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.720
then shifting future savings into the TFSA once income and the refund value drop.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.180
Implementation requires tracking adjusted cost-base across both accounts and using

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:33.720
a tax-planning spreadsheet or wealth-simple tax to model the refund differential each year.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.840
The strategy has historically added one to two percentage points of after-tax compounding

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.300
for investors whose income crosses brackets multiple times over a decade.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:51.820
Risk lies in over-contributing to the RRSP and facing withdrawal taxes if cash needs arise

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:57.540
before retirement. If you want to test this in your own accounts, start by running last year's

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.140
numbers through a simple spreadsheet that compares the marginal rate at contribution versus the

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:09.540
expected rate at withdrawal. That same discipline of waiting for confirmation shows up in our practice

